(+ & numbers) "Adds the numbers; (+) is 0."
(- number & numbers) "Subtracts the rest of the numbers from the first, or negates a single number: (- 10 3) is 7 and (- 4) is -4."
(* & numbers) "Multiplies the numbers; (*) is 1."
(/ number & numbers) "Divides the first number by the rest, or gives 1 over a single number, always as a float: (/ 6 2) is 3.0 and (/ 7 2) is 3.5. int or floor make a whole number of it."
(// a b) "Divides a by b and rounds down to a whole number: (// 7 2) is 3 and (// -7 2) is -4."
(mod a b) "The remainder of a divided by b, with the sign of b: (mod -7 3) is 2."
(rem a b) "The remainder of a divided by b, with the sign of a: (rem -7 3) is -1."
(inc x) "Adds one to x."
(dec x) "Subtracts one from x."
(abs x) "x without its sign."
(min number & numbers) "The smallest of the numbers."
(max number & numbers) "The largest of the numbers."
(round x) "Rounds x to the nearest whole number, halves away from zero."
(floor x) "Rounds x down to a whole number."
(ceil x) "Rounds x up to a whole number."
(int x) "Drops the fraction of x, rounding toward zero: (int 3.9) is 3."
(float x) "x as a float: (float 3) is 3.0."
(zero? x) "True when the number x is zero."
(pos? x) "True when the number x is above zero."
(neg? x) "True when the number x is below zero."
(even? x) "True when the whole number x is even."
(odd? x) "True when the whole number x is odd."
(== number & numbers) "True when the numbers are all numerically equal, so (== 1 1.0) is true where (= 1 1.0) is false."
(< number & numbers) "True when each number is less than the next: (< 1 2 3) is true."
(<= number & numbers) "True when each number is at most the next."
(> number & numbers) "True when each number is greater than the next."
(>= number & numbers) "True when each number is at least the next."

(identity x) "Returns x."
(constantly x) "A function that ignores its arguments and always returns x."
(not x) "True when x is false or nil."
(nil? x) "True when x is nil."
(some? x) "True when x is anything but nil."
(complement f) "A function that returns the opposite truth of f: ((complement even?) 3) is true."
(comp & fs) "A function that runs the functions right to left, each on the result of the next: ((comp inc abs) -3) is 4."
(partial f & fixed) "f with its first arguments filled in: ((partial + 10) 5) is 15."
(juxt & fs) "A function that calls each of fs with its arguments and returns their results in a vector: ((juxt first last) [1 2 3]) is [1 3]."
(memoize f) "f remembering its results, so calling it again with the same arguments returns the first answer."

(= value & values) "True when the values are all equal. Collections are equal when their items are; numbers of different types are not: use == for that."
(not= value & values) "True when the values are not all equal."
(identical? a b) "True when a and b are the very same value, not just equal ones."
(compare a b) "-1, 0 or 1 as a sorts before, with or after b. Numbers, strings, keywords, symbols, characters and sequences of them compare; nil sorts first."
(hash value) "A whole number such that equal values hash the same."
(type value) "The type of value as a keyword: :int, :float, :string, :keyword, :symbol, :list, :vector, :map, :set, :fn, :nil, :bool, :char, :atom, :promise, :generator, :channel, or a record's own type name."
(int? value) "True when value is a whole number."
(float? value) "True when value is a float."
(number? value) "True when value is a whole number or a float."
(string? value) "True when value is a string."
(char? value) "True when value is a character."
(keyword? value) "True when value is a keyword, such as :name or name:."
(symbol? value) "True when value is a symbol."
(bool? value) "True when value is true or false."
(list? value) "True when value is a list, including the empty list nil."
(fn? value) "True when value is a function, made with def or fn or built in. Keywords, maps and sets can be called too, but are not functions."
(keyword name) "The keyword with the given name, from a string, symbol or keyword: (keyword \"size\") is :size."
(symbol name) "The symbol with the given name, from a string, symbol or keyword."
(name value) "The name of a keyword or symbol as a string, without the colon: (name :size) is \"size\". Strings come back as they are."

(str & values) "Joins the values into one string, showing strings and characters as they are and leaving out nil: (str \"n = \" 3) is \"n = 3\"."
(pr-str value) "value written the way it reads back, with strings in quotes, as the REPL shows it."
(print & values) "Writes the values to standard output, separated by spaces."
(println & values) "Writes the values to standard output, separated by spaces, then a newline."

(atom value) "A mutable box holding value. Read it with @box or deref and change it with swap! or reset!."
(atom? value) "True when value is an atom."
(deref box) "The current value of an atom, also written @box."
(reset! box value) "Puts value in the atom box and returns it."
(compare-and-set! box old new) "Puts new in the atom box only when it still holds old, the very same value, and says whether it did."
(swap! a f & args) "Changes the atom a to (f current args...), retrying if another task changed it meanwhile, and returns the new value."

(first coll) "The first item of a collection, or nil when it is empty."
(second coll) "The second item of a collection, or nil."
(last coll) "The last item of a collection, or nil when it is empty."
(rest coll) "Everything after the first item: a list for a list, a vector for anything else."
(next generator) "Runs a generator to its next yield and returns the yielded value; after the last one, the generator is done?."
(nth coll index) "The item at index, counting from 0, in a vector, list or string. Raises a range-error when there is none; get returns nil instead."
(get coll key (default)) "The value under key in a map or record, the item at a position in a vector or string, or the member of a set; default, or nil, when there is none."
(count coll) "How many items a collection holds, or characters a string has."
(empty? coll) "True when a collection or string has nothing in it."
(contains? coll key) "True when a map has key or a set has key as a member. For vectors use some."
(keys m) "The keys of a map, as a vector."
(vals m) "The values of a map, as a vector."
(entries m) "The [key value] pairs of a map, as a vector."
(range n (end) (step)) "A vector of whole numbers: (range 4) is [0 1 2 3], (range 2 5) is [2 3 4] and (range 0 10 3) is [0 3 6 9]. The end is left out."
(concat & colls) "One vector with the items of every collection in order."
(reverse coll) "The items of a collection in reverse order, as a vector."
(cons item coll) "A list with item in front of the items of coll."
(conj coll & items) "Adds items to a collection where it grows best: at the end of a vector, at the front of a list, as members of a set, and as [key value] pairs or maps merged into a map."
(into target coll) "Adds every item of coll to target as conj would: (into [0] (list 1 2)) is [0 1 2]."
(assoc coll key value & more) "A copy of a map with key set to value, of a vector with the item at index key replaced, or of a record with a field changed. More key value pairs may follow."
(dissoc m & keys) "A copy of a map without the given keys."
(disj s & items) "A copy of a set without the given items."
(with record & fields) "A copy of a record with some fields changed: (with point :x 3)."
(vector & items) "A vector of the items."
(list & items) "A list of the items."
(set coll) "A set of the items of a collection."
(pop coll) "A vector without its last item, or a list without its first."
(peek coll) "The last item of a vector, or the first of a list: the one pop takes away."
(map? value) "True when value is a map."
(vector? value) "True when value is a vector."
(set? value) "True when value is a set."
(record? value) "True when value is a record."
(vec coll) "The items of a collection, or everything a generator yields, as a vector."
(take n coll) "The first n items of a collection or generator, as a vector."
(map f coll & colls) "A vector of f applied to each item of coll, or to the items of several collections side by side until the shortest runs out: (map + [1 2] [10 20]) is [11 22]."
(filter keep? coll) "A vector of the items of coll for which keep? is truthy."
(remove drop? coll) "A vector of the items of coll for which drop? is falsy."
(reduce f & args) "Combines the items of a collection with f, left to right, starting from init when given: (reduce + 0 [1 2 3]) is 6. Called as (reduce f coll), it starts from the first item."
(some pred coll) "The first truthy result of pred on the items of coll, or nil."
(every? pred coll) "True when pred is truthy for every item of coll."
(keep f coll) "A vector of the results of f on the items of coll, leaving out nil."
(find pred coll) "The first item of coll for which pred is truthy, or nil."
(sort-by key coll) "The items of coll sorted by (key item), keeping the order of ties."
(mapcat f coll & colls) "Maps f over the collections like map and concatenates the results."
(group-by key coll) "A map from each (key item) to a vector of the items that gave it."
(take-while keep? coll) "The items of a collection or generator up to the first for which keep? is falsy."
(drop-while drop? coll) "The items of coll from the first for which drop? is falsy."
(iterate f x) "A generator of x, (f x), (f (f x)) and so on, without end: (take 3 (iterate inc 0)) is [0 1 2]."
(drop n coll) "The items of coll after the first n, as a vector."
(distinct coll) "The items of coll without repeats, in their first order."
(chunk n coll) "The items of coll in vectors of n, the last one perhaps shorter: (chunk 2 [1 2 3]) is [[1 2] [3]]."
(partition n coll) "The items of coll in vectors of exactly n, leaving out any short last group."
(flatten coll) "The items of nested vectors and lists, in one flat vector."
(frequencies coll) "A map from each distinct item of coll to how many times it appears."
(zip & colls) "Vectors of the items of the collections side by side: (zip [1 2] [:a :b]) is [[1 :a] [2 :b]]."
(interleave & colls) "One item from each collection in turn: (interleave [1 2] [:a :b]) is [1 :a 2 :b]."
(repeat n x) "A vector of x, n times."
(get-in m path & default) "The value found by following the keys of path into nested maps and vectors, or default, or nil."
(assoc-in m path value) "A copy of nested maps m with value set at the keys of path, making maps on the way as needed."
(update coll k f & args) "A copy of coll with the value under k changed to (f value args...)."
(update-in m path f & args) "A copy of nested maps m with the value at path changed to (f value args...)."
(merge & maps) "One map with the entries of all the maps, later ones winning."
(merge-with f & maps) "Merges the maps like merge, combining the values of a key found in more than one with f."
(select-keys m wanted) "A map of just the entries of m whose keys are in wanted."
(sort coll) "The items of a collection in ascending order, as a vector. Raises a type-error for items that can't be compared."
(apply f first & more) "Calls f with the arguments, the last of which is a collection whose items are passed one by one: (apply + 1 [2 3]) is 6."
(expand-rules name literals rules form) "Expands form with the first rule whose pattern matches it; defrule and defrules build macros with it."

(raise error) "Raises error, which unwinds to the nearest try with a catch for its type. (raise (error \"msg\")) raises a plain error."

(promise) "A promise that someone can deliver a value to, or fail, later. await waits for it."
(deliver p value) "Fulfills the promise p with value, waking whatever awaits it."
(fail p error) "Fails the promise p with error, which is raised in whatever awaits it."
(await p) "Waits for the promise p and returns its value, or raises its error; any other value comes back as it is. Other tasks run meanwhile."
(promise? value) "True when value is a promise."
(done? value) "True when a generator has finished, a promise has settled, or a channel is closed and empty."
(generator f) "A generator that runs f, pausing at each yield; next resumes it. gen is the macro most code uses."
(generator? value) "True when value is a generator."
(yield (value)) "Pauses the running generator, handing value to whoever called next."
(spawn-call f) "Runs f as a task of its own and returns a promise of its result. The spawn macro wraps a body in a function for you."
(sleep ms) "Pauses the current task for ms milliseconds while other tasks run."
(every ms f) "Calls f every ms milliseconds in a task of its own, until f returns :stop."
(chan (size)) "A channel that tasks can send values through, holding up to size of them before send waits; 32 by default."
(chan? value) "True when value is a channel."
(send channel value) "Puts value on the channel, waiting while it is full."
(receive channel) "Takes the next value from the channel, waiting while it is empty. Returns nil once the channel is closed and empty."
(close channel) "Closes the channel: no more sends, and receive returns nil once it is drained."
(worker module function & arguments) "Runs function from module on a thread of its own with a fresh isolate and returns a promise of its result. Arguments and the result must be plain data."
(atomic-enter) "Stops other tasks from running until atomic-leave. Use the atomically macro instead."
(atomic-leave) "Lets other tasks run again after atomic-enter."
(profiler-start) "Starts sampling where Ox spends its time, returning true when this call started it. profile is the easy way in."
(profiler-stop) "Stops the profiler and returns its report as a map, or nil when it wasn't running."
(profiler-snapshot) "The profiler's report so far, as a map, while it keeps running; nil when it isn't."
(profile work) "Calls work while profiling and returns the report as a map; its :report is the printed table."
(profile-report found) "Prints the :report of a profile."
(shown-plainly? form) "True when form reads the same as its value, so expect doesn't repeat it."
(explain form values) "The text expect shows when form failed: the form, then what each argument evaluated to."

(regex pattern) "Compiles pattern into a regex once, for reuse. The regex/ functions also take pattern strings."
(regex/matches? pattern text) "True when the regex pattern matches anywhere in text."
(regex/find pattern text) "The first match of pattern in text, or nil, as a map of :text, :start, :end, :groups and :named groups."
(regex/find-all pattern text) "Every match of pattern in text, as a vector of match maps like regex/find returns."
(regex/replace pattern text with) "Replaces every match of pattern in text with with, where $1 or $name refer to groups."
(regex/split pattern text) "Splits text wherever pattern matches."
(image/open path) "A promise of the image in the file at path: PNG, JPEG, WebP, GIF, BMP, TIFF, ICO, QOI or SVG, turned upright by its EXIF orientation."
(image/probe path) "A promise of the :width, :height and :format of the image at path, read without decoding it."
(image/save image path {quality 90 format nil}) "A promise of path once image is written there, in the format its extension or :format names; :quality from 1 to 100 applies to JPEG. Metadata is not written."
(image/new width height {color nil}) "A new image width by height pixels, filled with :color or transparent."
(image/info image) "The :width, :height, source :format and whether the image had :alpha, as a map."
(image/width image) "The width of image in pixels."
(image/height image) "The height of image in pixels."
(image/image? value) "True when value is an image."
(image/color color) "color as [red green blue alpha]: a hex string such as \"#ff8800\", a vector, a CSS name such as :teal, :transparent, or a theme face such as :keyword."
(image/pixel image x y) "The color of the pixel at x, y as [red green blue alpha]."
(image/resize image width {height nil filter :lanczos}) "A promise of image scaled to width, and to :height or in proportion; :filter is :nearest, :linear, :cubic, :gaussian or :lanczos."
(image/fit image width height {filter :lanczos}) "A promise of image scaled in proportion to fit inside width by height."
(image/fill image width height {filter :lanczos}) "A promise of image scaled in proportion to cover width by height, then cropped to it around the center."
(image/thumbnail image size) "A promise of a quick small copy of image, at most size pixels on its longer side."
(image/crop image x y width height) "A promise of the part of image width by height from x, y, cut short at its edges."
(image/pad image amount {top nil right nil bottom nil left nil color nil}) "A promise of image with amount pixels added on each side, or :top, :right, :bottom and :left of their own, filled with :color or transparent; negative amounts cut instead."
(image/canvas image width height {anchor :center color nil}) "A promise of image placed on a width by height canvas at :anchor, such as :center, :top-left or :bottom, without scaling, filling the rest with :color or transparent."
(image/rotate image degrees {color nil expand true}) "A promise of image turned clockwise by degrees, on a canvas grown to fit unless :expand is false; new corners take :color or transparent."
(image/flip image direction) "A promise of image mirrored :horizontal or :vertical."
(image/trim image {tolerance 0}) "A promise of image with the borders that match its top-left color, within :tolerance per channel, cut away."
(image/affine image matrix {color nil}) "A promise of image moved by the affine matrix [a b c d e f], taking x, y to a x + b y + c, d x + e y + f."
(image/perspective image from to {color nil}) "A promise of image warped so the four [x y] points from land on the four points to."
(image/brightness image factor) "A promise of image with its colors multiplied by factor, as in CSS: 1 leaves it unchanged, 0 makes it black."
(image/contrast image factor) "A promise of image with its contrast scaled by factor around middle gray: 1 leaves it unchanged, 0 makes it gray."
(image/gamma image gamma) "A promise of image with gamma correction applied; above 1 lightens the midtones, below 1 darkens them."
(image/exposure image stops) "A promise of image brightened by stops of exposure, each doubling the light; negative stops darken."
(image/hue image degrees) "A promise of image with its hues turned by degrees around the color wheel, as in CSS hue-rotate."
(image/saturation image factor) "A promise of image with its saturation scaled by factor: 0 is gray, 1 unchanged, above 1 more vivid."
(image/vibrance image factor) "A promise of image with muted colors saturated by factor and already vivid ones left mostly alone."
(image/grayscale image (strength)) "A promise of image turned to gray, fully or by strength from 0 to 1."
(image/sepia image (strength)) "A promise of image toned sepia, fully or by strength from 0 to 1."
(image/invert image (strength)) "A promise of image with its colors inverted, fully or by strength from 0 to 1; alpha is kept."
(image/tint image color (strength)) "A promise of image multiplied by color, fully or by strength from 0 to 1, like a photo filter."
(image/balance image {red 1 green 1 blue 1 neutral nil}) "A promise of image with each channel scaled by its factor; :neutral names a color that should come out gray, to fix white balance."
(image/levels image {black 0 white 255 gamma 1 output-black 0 output-white 255}) "A promise of image with the :black to :white input range stretched to the output range, bending midtones by :gamma."
(image/curves image points {channel :all}) "A promise of image with its tones mapped through a smooth curve passing through the [in out] points, each from 0 to 255; :channel is :all, :red, :green, :blue or :alpha."
(image/posterize image levels) "A promise of image with each channel cut to levels evenly spaced values."
(image/threshold image (level)) "A promise of image in black and white: pixels lighter than level, 128 by default, turn white; :otsu picks the level from the image."
(image/replace image from to {tolerance 0}) "A promise of image with every pixel within :tolerance per channel of color from replaced by color to."
(image/quantize image palette {dither true}) "A promise of image reduced to the colors in a palette vector, or to that many colors picked from the image by median cut, with Floyd-Steinberg dithering unless :dither is false."
(image/blur image radius) "A promise of image with a Gaussian blur whose standard deviation is radius pixels, as in CSS blur; transparent edges don't darken."
(image/box-blur image radius) "A promise of image with each pixel averaged over a square reaching radius pixels around it."
(image/median image radius) "A promise of image with each channel replaced by its median over a square reaching radius pixels, which removes speckles and keeps edges."
(image/pixelate image size) "A promise of image made of size by size blocks, each the average of its pixels."
(image/sharpen image (amount)) "A promise of image sharpened by amount, 1 by default, using an unsharp mask of radius 1."
(image/unsharp image radius amount {threshold 0}) "A promise of image sharpened with an unsharp mask: the difference from a blur of radius is added back amount times, where it is at least :threshold."
(image/edges image {method :sobel low 20 high 50}) "A promise of a grayscale map of image's edges, light where the image changes: :sobel shades by strength, :canny draws thin lines between its :low and :high thresholds."
(image/emboss image (strength)) "A promise of image embossed, as if pressed into metal, fully or by strength from 0 to 1."
(image/convolve image kernel {divisor nil offset 0}) "A promise of image with every color channel convolved with kernel, a vector of rows with an odd width and height, divided by :divisor, the kernel's sum by default, then :offset added."
(image/noise image amount {seed 0 mono false}) "A promise of image with Gaussian grain whose standard deviation is amount in channel values; the same :seed gives the same grain, and :mono grain is gray."
(image/vignette image (strength) {radius 0.5 color :black}) "A promise of image fading to :color toward its corners, starting :radius of the way out from the center."
(image/begin-edit image) "A canvas holding a private copy of image, for drawing in place until image/end-edit; image/edit does both around a function."
(image/end-edit canvas) "The image drawn on canvas, ending its edit so it can't be drawn on again."
(image/edit image change) "Calls change with a canvas holding a private copy of image, so the drawing functions ending in ! can change it in place, then returns the edited image."
(image/set-pixel! target x y color) "Sets the pixel at x, y to color, in place on a canvas, or as a promise of a new image when target is an image."
(image/rect! target x y w h {fill nil stroke nil width 1 opacity 1 blend :normal cap :butt join :miter dash nil radius 0}) "Draws a w by h rectangle from x, y, filled with :fill, black when neither :fill nor :stroke is given, outlined with :stroke :width wide, its corners rounded by :radius. Like every drawing function ending in !, it changes a canvas in place and returns it, or gives a promise of a new image when target is an image."
(image/ellipse! target x y rx ry {fill nil stroke nil width 1 opacity 1 blend :normal cap :butt join :miter dash nil}) "Draws an ellipse centered on x, y with radii rx and ry, styled like image/rect!."
(image/circle! target x y radius {fill nil stroke nil width 1 opacity 1 blend :normal cap :butt join :miter dash nil}) "Draws a circle centered on x, y, styled like image/rect!."
(image/polygon! target points {fill nil stroke nil width 1 opacity 1 blend :normal cap :butt join :miter dash nil}) "Draws the closed shape through a vector of [x y] points, styled like image/rect!."
(image/line! target x1 y1 x2 y2 {color :black width 1 opacity 1 blend :normal cap :butt join :miter dash nil}) "Draws a line from x1, y1 to x2, y2 in :color, :width wide, with :cap :butt, :round or :square ends and a :dash vector of on and off lengths."
(image/polyline! target points {color :black width 1 opacity 1 blend :normal cap :butt join :miter dash nil}) "Draws connected lines through a vector of [x y] points, styled like image/line!, with :join :miter, :round or :bevel corners."
(image/paste! target image x y {blend :normal opacity 1 mask nil scale 1}) "Draws image over target with its top-left corner at x, y, scaled by :scale, mixed by :blend, any CSS blend mode such as :multiply, :screen, :overlay, :soft-light, :difference or :luminosity, at :opacity, showing only where the :mask image is light."
(image/mask image mask) "A promise of image with its alpha multiplied by how light mask is, stretched to fit."
(image/tile image width height) "A promise of a width by height image covered with copies of image."

(string/length text) "How many characters text has."
(string/upper text) "Returns text in upper case."
(string/lower text) "Returns text in lower case."
(string/capitalize text) "text with its first character in upper case."
(string/trim text) "text without whitespace at either end."
(string/trim-start text) "text without whitespace at the start."
(string/trim-end text) "text without whitespace at the end."
(string/blank? text) "True when text is empty or only whitespace."
(string/starts-with? text prefix) "True when text starts with prefix."
(string/ends-with? text suffix) "True when text ends with suffix."
(string/includes? text part) "True when part appears in text."
(string/index-of text part) "Where part first appears in text, counting characters from 0, or nil."
(string/split text separator (limit)) "Splits text at each separator into a vector of strings, into at most limit pieces when given."
(string/join separator-or-items (items)) "Joins the items into one string, with separator between them when one is given: (string/join \", \" [1 2]) is \"1, 2\"."
(string/lines text) "The lines of text, without their line endings."
(string/words text) "The words of text, split at whitespace."
(string/chars text) "The characters of text, as a vector."
(string/replace text from to) "text with every from replaced by to."
(string/repeat text times) "text repeated times times."
(string/reverse text) "text backwards."
(string/slice text start (end)) "The characters of text from start up to end, or to its end, counting from 0."
(string/truncate text width) "text cut to width characters, ending in … when it was longer."
(string/pad-start text width (fill)) "text with fill, or spaces, added in front until it is width characters long."
(string/pad-end text width (fill)) "text with fill, or spaces, added after it until it is width characters long."
(string/to-number text) "The number text spells, or nil: (string/to-number \"2.5\") is 2.5."

(path/join part & parts) "Joins path parts with the system's separator."
(path/parent path) "The folder holding path, or nil."
(path/name path) "The last part of path, such as notes.txt."
(path/stem path) "The last part of path without its extension, such as notes."
(path/extension path) "The extension of path without the dot, such as txt, or nil."
(path/home) "The user's home folder."
(path/expand path) "path with a leading ~ replaced by the home folder."
(path/abbreviate path) "path with the home folder shown as ~."
(path/absolute? path) "True when path doesn't depend on the current folder."
(path/separator) "The system's path separator, / or \\."
(path/temp) "The system's folder for temporary files."

(file/read path) "A promise of the contents of the file at path, as a string. Use await to get it."
(file/write path text) "Writes text to the file at path, replacing what was there, and returns a promise that settles when it is done."
(file/append path text) "Adds text to the end of the file at path, making the file when it is missing, and returns a promise that settles when it is done."
(file/read-base64 path) "A promise of the bytes of the file at path, written as base64, for files that aren't text."
(file/write-base64 path encoded) "Writes the bytes base64 text encodes to the file at path, replacing what was there; returns a promise."
(file/exists? path) "A promise of whether anything exists at path."
(file/list folder) "A promise of the entries in folder, each a map of :name, :path, :dir, :link, :target, :hidden, :size and :modified."
(file/metadata path) "A promise of a map describing path, with the same fields file/list gives."
(file/make-dir path) "Creates the folder at path and any missing parents; returns a promise."
(file/rename from to) "Moves or renames from to to; returns a promise."
(file/copy from to) "Copies a file, or a folder and everything in it, from from to to; returns a promise."
(file/remove path) "Deletes the file or folder at path for good; returns a promise. file/trash is the gentler choice."
(file/trash path) "Moves the file or folder at path to the system's trash; returns a promise."

(process/run program arguments {cwd nil env nil input nil}) "Runs program with a vector of arguments and returns a promise of a map of :status, :stdout and :stderr. :cwd sets its folder, :env a map of variables to set or, with nil, remove, and :input text for its standard input."
(process/env name) "The value of the environment variable name, or nil."
(process/which program) "The path of program in the folders of PATH, trying program.exe first on Windows, or nil when it isn't there."
(process/os) "The operating system as a keyword: :windows, :macos, :linux or another name Rust gives it."
(process/start program arguments {cwd nil env nil input nil}) "Starts program and returns at once a map of :lines and :errors, channels of its output and error lines that close when it ends, and :exit, a promise of its exit status. It raises when the program can't start."
(process/write process text) "Writes text to the standard input of a started process; false once its input is closed."
(process/close-input process) "Closes the standard input of a started process, which many programs take as the end of their work."
(process/kill process) "Stops a started process at once."

(json/read text) "The value that JSON text describes: objects become maps with keyword keys, and arrays vectors."
(json/write value) "value as JSON text. Map keys must be keywords or strings."
(xml/parse text) "The root element of XML text, as a map of :tag, its name; :attrs, a map of its attributes; and :children, a vector of its child elements and text. Names lose their namespace prefixes, text that is only whitespace is left out, and so are comments."
(xml/find element path) "The first element along path below element, child tag names joined by /, such as \"channel/item\"; nil when there is none or element is nil."
(xml/find-all element path) "Every element along path below element, such as \"channel/item\", in document order."
(xml/text element) "All the text inside element and its descendants, joined; a string is its own text, and nil gives nil."
(xml/attr element name) "The value of element's attribute name, given as a string or keyword; nil when it has none."
(xml/write element) "element, a map like those xml/parse makes, as XML text, with its text and attribute values escaped."

(http/get url) "A promise of the response to a GET of url, as a map of :status, :headers and :body."
(http/encode text) "text made safe for a URL, with other characters written as %XX."
(http/request method url {headers nil body nil user nil password nil}) "A promise of the response to a request with any method, such as :put or :propfind, as a map of :status, :headers and :body. :headers is a map, :body the text to send, and :user and :password log in with HTTP basic authentication."
(net/connect host port {tls false}) "A promise of a connection to host on port, encrypted with :tls true: a map whose :lines channel gives each line the server sends and is closed when the connection ends."
(net/send connection line) "Sends line to the server, ending it with CR LF; false when the connection has ended."
(net/close connection) "Ends the connection once what was sent has gone out."
(crypto/random count) "count random bytes from the system, written as hex."
(crypto/random-below bound) "A random whole number from 0 up to but not including bound, every one equally likely."
(crypto/derive-key password salt {memory 65536 iterations 3 parallelism 1}) "A promise of a key made from password and a hex salt with Argon2id, using :memory KiB, as hex. The same password and salt always give the same key."
(crypto/seal key text) "text encrypted with key using XChaCha20-Poly1305 and a fresh random nonce, as base64."
(crypto/open key sealed) "The text crypto/seal sealed with key; raises when the key is wrong or the text was changed."
(crypto/totp secret {time nil step 30 digits 6}) "The time-based one-time password for a base32 secret, as authenticator apps show it, for :time in seconds or now."
(crypto/sha256 text) "The SHA-256 digest of text, as hex."
(crypto/to-base64 text) "text written as base64."
(crypto/from-base64 encoded) "The text that base64 encoded holds."
(csv/read text {header false}) "The rows of comma-separated text as vectors of strings, with quoted fields that may hold commas, quotes and new lines. With :header true the first row names the fields and each other row becomes a map from those names."

(time/now) "Milliseconds since the start of 1970, UTC."
(time/local millis (zone)) "The local date and time of a moment, or its time in zone such as \"UTC\" or \"Europe/Paris\", as a map of :year, :month, :day, :hour, :minute, :second and :weekday."
(time/instant given {zone nil}) "The moment, in milliseconds since 1970, that given names: text like 2026-10-23T05:43:15Z or with an offset, or a date and time like 2026-10-23T09:30 or a map like time/local gives, read in :zone or the local zone."
(time/add millis amounts {zone nil}) "The moment amounts after millis, with amounts a map of :years, :months, :weeks, :days, :hours, :minutes and :seconds, any of them negative. Days and longer follow the calendar of :zone or the local zone, so a day across a daylight saving change is still a day."
(time/format millis pattern {zone nil}) "The moment written with a strftime pattern such as \"%H:%M\" or \"%A %e %B %Y\", in :zone or the local zone."
(time/days-in-month year month) "How many days month has in year."
(time/zone) "The name of the local time zone, such as \"Europe/Paris\", or \"UTC\" when it has none."
(time/parse text) "The date or date and time that text spells, such as 2026-10-22 or 2026-10-22T09:30, as a map like time/local gives."
(time/millis n) "n milliseconds, in milliseconds."
(time/seconds n) "n seconds, in milliseconds: (sleep (time/seconds 2))."
(time/minutes n) "n minutes, in milliseconds."
(time/hours n) "n hours, in milliseconds."
(time/days n) "n days, in milliseconds."

(when test & body) "Evaluates body when test is truthy; otherwise nil."
(unless test & body) "Evaluates body when test is falsy; otherwise nil."
(and & forms) "Evaluates forms left to right until one is falsy and returns it, or the last value; (and) is true."
(or & forms) "Evaluates forms left to right until one is truthy and returns it; otherwise the last value, and (or) is nil."
(cond & clauses) "Tries (test body...) clauses in turn and evaluates the body of the first whose test is truthy; an else clause always matches."
(case value & clauses) "Evaluates the body of the first [constant body...] clause whose constant equals value, or nil when none does. Constants aren't evaluated, a list of them matches any one, and an else clause always matches: (case key [:up 1] [(:left :right) 2] [else 3])."
(each [name coll ...] & body) "Evaluates body for each item of coll, bound to name, and returns nil. More name and coll pairs nest, :when test skips items and :let [bindings] names more values: (each [line lines :when (not (empty? line))] (println line))."
(for [name coll ...] & body) "A vector of body's value for each item of coll, bound to name. More pairs nest, :when test skips items and :let [bindings] names more values: (for [x [1 2 3] :when (odd? x)] (* x x)) is [1 9]."
(while test & body) "Evaluates body again and again while test is truthy, and returns nil."
(until test & body) "Evaluates body again and again until test is truthy, and returns nil."
(-> value & forms) "Threads value through the forms as their first argument: (-> x (f 1) g) is (g (f x 1))."
(->> value & forms) "Threads value through the forms as their last argument: (->> xs (map f) (filter g)) is (filter g (map f xs))."
(some-> value & forms) "Threads value through the forms like ->, stopping with nil as soon as a step gives nil."
(as-> value name & forms) "Threads value through the forms, binding it to name in each so it can go anywhere: (as-> 5 n (- 10 n) (* n 2)) is 10."
(if-let binding then & otherwise) "Like if, binding [name value] for then when value is truthy."
(when-let binding & body) "Like when, binding [name value] for body when value is truthy."
(defrule pattern template) "Defines a macro by example: calls that match pattern become template, with ... repeating what it follows."
(defrules name literals & rules) "Defines a macro from [pattern template] rules, using the first that matches; literals are words that match only themselves."
(maybe form) "The value of form, or nil if it raises an error."
(atomically & body) "Evaluates body without letting other tasks run in between."
(gen & body) "A generator that runs body, pausing at each yield: (vec (gen (yield 1) (yield 2))) is [1 2]."
(spawn & body) "Runs body as a task of its own and returns a promise of its result."
(test title & body) "Registers a test called title that ox test runs; it passes unless body raises, and expect raises on a false check."
(expect form) "Checks that form is truthy, raising expectation-failed with what each argument was when it isn't: (expect (= (area 2 3) 6))."

(special
 (if test then (else)) "Evaluates then when test is truthy and else, or nil, otherwise. Only false and nil are falsy."
 (do & body) "Evaluates each form of body in order and returns the last value."
 (def name (doc) value) "Defines name in this module. (def (name params) doc body...) defines a function; a string first in the body is its doc."
 (defonce name value) "Defines name like def, but only when it isn't defined yet, so reloading keeps its value."
 (fn (name) [params] & body) "A function. Parameters may be optional, as (name default), take the rest after &, or come last as keywords in a map of defaults."
 (let [name value ...] & body) "Binds each name to its value in turn, and evaluates body with them. Names may be patterns that destructure."
 (loop [name value ...] & body) "Binds each name like let and evaluates body, where (again value ...) as the last thing it does goes round once more with new values: (loop [i 0 total 0] (if (< i 4) (again (inc i) (+ total i)) total)) is 6."
 (again & values) "Inside loop, as the last thing it does: starts the loop's body again with its names bound to values, one for each."
 (set! name value) "Changes the value of a local or a global."
 (. value :field & fields) "Reads a field of a record or map, and fields of that in turn. value.field is the short form."
 (quote form) "form itself, unevaluated; also written 'form."
 (quasiquote form) "form as data, written `form, with ~value filled in and ~@values spliced in."
 (defmacro name [params] (doc) & body) "Defines a macro: a function that runs when code is read and returns the code to use instead."
 (defrecord Name [fields]) "Defines a record type and its constructor, (Name field ...). Fields are read with record.field."
 (deferror name [fields] (message)) "Defines an error type that raise can raise and catch can catch, with a message that may use its fields, keyword fields with their defaults included."
 (match value & clauses) "Tries each [pattern body...] clause in turn and evaluates the body of the first whose pattern matches value. A clause may add :when test. Raises a no-match-error when none does."
 (try & body) "Evaluates body. (catch type name body...) clauses handle errors of that type, and a (finally body...) clause runs whatever happens."
 (catch type name & body) "Inside try: handles an error of type, or of any type for error, binding it to name."
 (finally & body) "Inside try: runs after the body and any catch, whether or not an error was raised."
 (module & clauses) "The first form of a file: (import (ox string :as s)) and (import (lib names :only [f g])) bring in other modules, and (export f g) limits what this one shares.")
